        "resonator_obs autocorrelation at 8-day lag r=0.712",
        "pendulum_obs autocorrelation at 6-day lag r=0.400",
        "resonator_lab autocorrelation at 7-day lag r=0.509",
        "pendulum_lab autocorrelation at 5-day lag r=0.335"
      ],
      "evidenceAgainst": [
        "weak cross-instrument agreement (pendulum_lab ↔ resonator_lab r=0.207, pendulum_lab ↔ resonator_obs r=0.409)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "label": "Measurement procedure or observer bias introducing correlated noise unique to each setup",
      "probability": 0.35,
      "rationale": "The autocorrelations are strongest in the observational setups (pendulum_obs and resonator_obs), which may involve more human intervention, subjective timing, or environmental sensitivity than the lab setups. Bias or procedural drift could generate spurious periodicity. However, the lab instruments (pendulum_lab, resonator_lab) also show significant autocorrelation, which weakens this hypothesis. The lack of exact repetition runs (all ≤1 of 60–108 readings) rules out simple copying or data fabrication. The minute value spacing (0.0001 resolution across all four instruments) suggests genuine measurement variance, not artificial rounding.",
      "evidenceFor": [
        "observational instruments show higher autocorrelations (0.400, 0.712) than lab instruments (0.335, 0.509)",
        "no suspicious exact repetition runs detected"
      ],
      "evidenceAgainst": [
        "lab instruments also show significant autocorrelation, not just observational ones",
        "high resolution and diversity in distinct values argue against systematic bias or rounding"
      ]
    },
    {
      "label": "Common environmental driver with propagation delay and/or nonlinear sensor response",
      "probability": 0.10,
      "rationale": "If a single physical phenomenon (e.g., ambient temperature, magnetic field, pressure cycle) drives all instruments but reaches them at different times or through different transduction pathways, cross-correlations could appear weak while each instrument shows lagged self-correlation. The varying lags (5–8 days) could reflect different time constants or phase lags in sensor response. However, this requires a very specific multipath coupling mechanism. The near-complete absence of strong cross-instrument correlation (best r=0.409 at 5-day offset, still below expected random chance of ±0.529) argues strongly against any simple common driver.",
      "evidenceFor": [
        "pendulum_lab ↔ resonator_obs r=0.409 at 5-day offset slightly exceeds chance ±0.529"
      ],
      "evidenceAgainst": [
        "all other cross-instrument correlations fall below or near chance level",
        "varying lag structure (5–8 days) not well explained by a single common driver",
        "no level changes or environmental event records documented"
      ]
    },
    {
      "label": "Statistical artifact: autocorrelation overstated due to small sample size and multiple comparisons",
      "probability": 0.05,
      "rationale": "Autocorrelation tests across many lags without correction for multiple comparisons can inflate false positives. Resonator_obs autocorrelation (n=12 pairs at 8-day lag) is particularly vulnerable to noise; resonator_lab (n=18) and pendulum_obs (n=24) only marginally more robust. However, resonator_obs r=0.712 exceeds ±0.837 chance bound, and pendulum_obs r=0.400 exceeds ±0.592 bound, suggesting real signal rather than pure noise. Pendulum_lab and resonator_lab are closer to chance. The pattern of stronger autocorrelations at different lags for different instruments argues against a universal artifact.",
      "evidenceFor": [
        "resonator_lab and pendulum_lab autocorrelations lie close to or within chance bounds"
      ],
      "evidenceAgainst": [
        "resonator_obs r=0.712 exceeds its chance bound ±0.837",
        "pendulum_obs r=0.400 exceeds its chance bound ±0.592"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "residual": 0.00,
  "summaryOfChange": "Previous state carried a residual of 1.00 (no hypotheses). Baseline data (days 1–10) now complete on all four instruments (240 trials total). The most striking anomaly is not drift or secular change, but systematic autocorrelation: each instrument's readings echo their own values from 5–8 days prior, while cross-instrument correlations remain uniformly weak. This is the opposite of what I would expect if all four instruments measured a common signal cleanly. I now prioritize explaining this autocorrelation structure. The leading hypothesis is that each instrument has its own slow periodic or drift-driven cycle, decoupled from the others—suggesting either instrumental artifacts, measurement procedure bias, or fundamental incompatibility in how the four setups transduce the target quantity. Weak cross-instrument agreement argues against a shared environmental driver. A common-driver hypothesis is relegated to 10% because the cross-correlations are so consistently poor. I retain a small allocation (5%) to statistical artifact, but resonator_obs and pendulum_obs exceed their chance bounds. I am now ready to move into a targeted post-baseline phase: (1) intentionally break and re-establish baselines on suspected instruments to test for hysteresis or setup-dependent bias; (2) attempt to synchronize observational and lab procedures to test whether procedural drift explains the difference; (3) continue all four instruments in parallel to accumulate drift data and refine lag estimates. No hypothesis is yet publishable; all require replication under varied conditions."
}
```

